 \section{Conclusion}
-Under ordered raw-neighbor suppression, deleting an excluded record can
-change the learner's training target. A cumulative deletion horizon gives
-both an exact repair rule and a precise boundary on which excluded
-payloads can still matter. The finite-format family shows that this
-boundary can change required private information sharply, even when all
-lower-budget heads are identical. Both fully LLM calibration versions
-failed their frozen selection criteria. Consequently, the downstream
-natural-text effect study remains unexecuted. Separate development
-diagnostics exhibit structural admissions and verify exact indexed repair
-on fixed score graphs; their practical NLP value remains unresolved.
+Exact repair after curation is a question about future training sets.
+The original deletion budget specifies which excluded records can still
+enter, and the remaining budget specifies which information may be
+discarded from the retained state. The coefficient construction makes
+this contract executable for additive statistics. The finite-feature
+family shows why the same contract can require information that the
+current model does not reveal. A practical deletion service should make
+its curation target, cumulative horizon, and retained-data access explicit
+before discarding the payloads needed to honor them.
 
